Build a Hive Desktop applet.
{{template "app" .}}
An applet is a web page that opens over the app, from the command palette or a key
the user binds. It runs on the user's own machine with their full access, and that
is intended: do what the request needs.

## Files

Write the applet to {{.Env.AppletsDir}}/<id>/. The directory name is the applet's id:
a slug of lowercase letters, digits and hyphens. It holds an `applet.yaml` and the
page. The app watches these files, so the applet appears as soon as the file is
valid. Nothing runs until the user opens it.

```yaml
version: 1
title: Containers
icon: container
width: 1200
height: 760
```

Unknown keys are errors.

- `version`: must be `1`.
- `title`: the name in the palette.
- `icon`: optional glyph: `terminal`, `git-branch`, `git-compare`, `folder`, `file-text`, `search`, `database`, `gauge`, `activity`, `flask-conical`, `hammer`, `container`, `cloud`, `bug`, `zap`, `package`, `globe`, `external-link`, `git-pull-request`, or `play`.
- `cwd`: where commands run. Omit it when the applet is about the code in front of the user: commands then run in the on-screen session's checkout, and in the applet's directory when no session is on screen. Set `"~"` or an absolute path when the applet works anywhere. A relative path is relative to the applet's directory.
- `entry`: the page, default `index.html`.
- `width` and `height`: the panel in pixels. The default fills the window.

## The page

It is a normal web page, served from the applet's directory: relative scripts, ES
modules, stylesheets, images, audio and network requests all work. There is no build
step, so either write plain files or commit the built output. Editing a page, script
or stylesheet reloads it on screen and stops the processes it started. Hive draws a
close button over the top right corner of the page, so leave about 52px clear there.

`window.hive` exists before your scripts run. Every call returns a promise.

- `hive.exec(command, {cwd, env, stdin, timeout})` runs a shell command and resolves to `{stdout, stderr, code}`. A non-zero `code` does not reject. `timeout` is in milliseconds and defaults to 5 minutes.
- `hive.spawn(command, {cwd, env, onStdout, onStderr, onExit})` starts a long-running process and resolves to `{write(text), kill()}`. Output arrives as text in arbitrary chunks. Closing the applet stops it.
- `hive.readFile(path)` and `hive.writeFile(path, text)` handle text files. A relative path is relative to the applet's directory, and `~` and absolute paths work.
- `hive.terminal(command, {cwd})` opens the command in the pop-up terminal, for anything interactive.
- `hive.toast(message, {severity, body})` shows a Hive toast. `severity` is `info`, `success`, `warning`, or `error`.
- `hive.copy(text)` puts text on the clipboard.
- `hive.context()` resolves to `{id, title, dir, cwd, sessionSlug}`.
- `hive.close()` closes the applet.

Commands run through `sh -c` in the directory `cwd` describes, with `$HIVE_APPLET_DIR`
set. Pass values to a command through `env` and quote them in the command
(`hive.exec('docker restart "$ID"', {env: {ID: id}})`), because a value built into the
command text is shell injection by accident.

`localStorage` works and persists per applet. Cookies and IndexedDB do not, because
the frame has an opaque origin. Read and write files for anything larger. A page can
navigate to another HTML file of its own, but cannot `fetch` one. If the frame
navigates to any other site, that page gets no `window.hive`.

Hive sets its theme on the page's root element as CSS variables, and updates them when
the user changes theme. They are empty for a moment while the page loads, so give each
`var()` a fallback: `--hv-app`, `--hv-sidebar`, `--hv-pane`, `--hv-raised`, `--hv-list`, `--hv-chip`, `--hv-hover`, `--hv-row`, `--hv-row-hover`, `--hv-card`, `--hv-border`, `--hv-strong`, `--hv-text`, `--hv-text-2`, `--hv-text-3`, `--hv-text-4`, `--hv-accent`, `--hv-accent-contrast`, `--hv-accent-tint`, `--hv-selection`, `--hv-backdrop`, `--hv-code-key`, `--hv-code-string`, `--hv-code-comment`, and `--hv-severity-info`, `-success`, `-warning`, `-error` and `-running`, each with a `-tint`.

Escape closes the applet unless your keydown handler calls `preventDefault()`. Use
that to let Escape back out of a dialog or a filter first. Keys typed in the page do
not reach Hive's own shortcuts. Audio needs a user gesture first, as on any page.

## Check your work

1. Run the commands the page uses and confirm their output is what the page parses.
2. If the `list_applets` tool of the app's MCP server is available, call it and fix any `error` it reports. Without it, the palette row for a broken applet opens and shows the same error.
3. Tell the user the applet's name, and that they open it from the command palette and can bind it to a key as `applet.<id>`.
